In Spring Framework, there are two primary types of IoC (Inversion of Control) containers: [1]

• BeanFactory:
	• This is the most basic and lightweight IoC container in Spring.
	• It provides fundamental support for dependency injection, managing beans, and their lifecycle.
	• Beans are typically loaded lazily, meaning they are instantiated only when requested.
	• It is suitable for resource-constrained environments or simple applications where advanced features are not required.

• ApplicationContext:
	• This is a more advanced and feature-rich IoC container that extends the functionality of BeanFactory.
	• It includes all the capabilities of BeanFactory and adds enterprise-specific features like:
		• Event propagation: Ability to publish and listen for application events.
		• Internationalization (I18n): Support for message resolution in different locales.
		• Spring AOP integration: Seamless integration with Aspect-Oriented Programming.
		• Declarative mechanisms: for creating beans and managing their lifecycle.

	• Beans are typically loaded eagerly (eager initialization), meaning they are instantiated at startup.
	• ApplicationContext is generally recommended for most enterprise applications due to its comprehensive features.
	• Common implementations include ClassPathXmlApplicationContext, FileSystemXmlApplicationContext, and WebXmlApplicationContext.

In essence:

• BeanFactory is a foundational, minimalist container.
• ApplicationContext is an enhanced container built upon BeanFactory, offering a broader range of functionalities for more complex applications.

